    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That's a good start, very clear. It's accurate too.\n\nCould you expand on this a bit more? I need a more detailed breakdown of the actual phases—prophase, metaphase, anaphase, and telophase. Maybe add a little bit about what's happening to the chromosomes in each stage.\n\nAgain, just keep it focused on the science for the students. I'm worried if we go too deep into detail, the automated tool might flag it as \"low engagement\" if it doesn't see enough of those instructional signposting words, so just try to keep it balanced without being weird about it.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Mitosis proceeds through distinct phases that dictate chromosome movement. During prophase, chromosomes condense and become visible, while the nuclear envelope breaks down. Next, in metaphase, these duplicated chromosomes line up neatly along the cell's equator. Anaphase involves the sister chromatids separating and being pulled to opposite ends of the cell. Finally, telophase reverses these steps, forming two new nuclei around the separated sets of genetic material.",
      "source": "target",
