    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Mitosis is crucial for cell replication, enabling organisms to grow and repair damaged tissues by creating exact duplicates. To truly understand this process, we must examine its four key phases to learn how genetic material is managed. In prophase, the cell prepares by condensing its chromatin into visible chromosomes. Metaphase is when the chromosomes line up precisely in the middle of the cell, acting like a metronome for division. Anaphase is the dramatic split, where sister chromatids are pulled apart to opposite poles. Lastly, telophase rebuilds the two separate sets of DNA into new, functional nuclei.",
      "source": "target",
